## Root cause

`objToLocalTS(obj)` in `/luxon/src/impl/util.js` (used by `DateTime#plus`/`minus` via `adjustTime` → `objToLocalTS`, and also by parsing, `set`, and IANAZone offsets).

`Date.UTC(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day, ...)` interprets years 0–99 as 1900+year, so the code corrects the year afterwards. The buggy correction was:

```js
d = new Date(d);
d.setUTCFullYear(d.getUTCFullYear() - 1900);
```

When the month/day fields overflow (e.g. `plus({days})` produces `{year: 99, month: 11, day: 122}`), `Date.UTC` first normalizes the overflow using year **2000** (a leap year, Feb = 29 days), then the code just rewrites the year to **100** (not a leap year, Feb = 28 days). The day-of-year was already computed with the wrong February, so the result is one day early. This is exactly "passing through February across the 100 barrier": `DateTime.fromISO("0099-12-31").plus({days: 61})` returned `0100-03-01` instead of `0100-03-02`. (Adding only 2 days, as in the existing test, never overflows into a different year, so that test passed.)

